  "brief_md": "# A13 — Email middleware for correct per-recipient alias sending\n\n## Idea\nMiddleware that watches which alias a message was received on and automatically sends replies/outgoing mail from that same alias, for privacy-conscious prosumers running many email aliases.\n\n## Payer\nIndividual privacy-conscious prosumers paying a personal subscription. Price hypothesis: $1-4/month, anchored to Maskwire's $3/month all-tools price (https://maskwire.com/), which already includes this exact feature as one of six named 'tools' ('Alias replies').\n\n## Competitors\n- **Maskwire** ($3/mo all tools, one free) — an IMAP/SMTP proxy that already ships 'Alias replies' as a named feature. https://maskwire.com/\n- **addy.io** (Free / $1 Lite / $3-4 Pro) — reply-and-send-from-alias included on all paid tiers. https://addy.io\n- **SimpleLogin** (Free / ~$3-4/mo Premium) — send/reply from alias with unlimited sends is core to the product. https://simplelogin.io/pricing/\n- **ProxiedMail** (Free / Plus / Forever lifetime) — 'Replies from proxy-email' included even on the free tier; outbound sending from proxy email on paid tier. https://proxiedmail.com/en/pricing\n- **Send As Alias** (free, open-source Thunderbird extension) — client-side from-address auto-correction including delivery-header detection for BCC/mailing-list cases. https://addons.thunderbird.net/en-US/thunderbird/addon/send-as-alias/\n\n## Acquisition channel\n- Reddit Ads targeting privacy subreddits: ~$0.50-$4.00 average CPC (https://www.stackmatix.com/blog/how-much-does-reddit-advertising-cost)\n- Niche privacy newsletter sponsorship: $50-250 per send for small indie newsletters (https://joinbreaker.ai/blog-posts/newsletter-ad-rates)\n\n## Demand evidence\nMultiple independent forum threads (Zoho, Apple, Microsoft, eM Client) over several years document users hitting exactly this pain — mail clients defaulting to the wrong From/Reply-To address when replying to an alias:\n- \"The email always sends with a Reply To address from the main domain, not the alias\" — https://help.zoho.com/portal/en-gb/community/topic/reply-to-email-addresses-wrong\n- \"When I reply to an email received to me@example.com, the mail app selects otherme@example.com, not the default\" — https://discussions.apple.com/thread/252756221\n- \"The 'from' address is set to main (my admin email). It should be replying from info\" — https://learn.microsoft.com/en-au/answers/questions/5651976/outlook-mac-app-reply-with-the-correct-alias-email\n\n## Case for\nThe core logic (map incoming recipient header to outgoing From/Reply-To) is small and well-specified, buildable solo with AI agents in six weeks, especially as a client-side plugin or browser extension rather than a full mail proxy. The pain is real, recurring, and documented across multiple major mail clients for years, suggesting an underserved segment that wants this one fix without a full alias-management suite.\n\n## Case against\nThis exact feature is already shipped and priced by at least five live competitors, including one (Maskwire) that sells it as a named $3/month tool today, and others (addy.io, SimpleLogin, ProxiedMail) that bundle it into their core free-to-cheap alias-forwarding products. The commodity nature and sub-$4/month price ceiling make unit economics tight for a standalone product, and a free, open-source Thunderbird extension already solves the client-side case for free.\n\n## Open questions\nSee `open_questions` field — chiefly whether users would pay for a standalone tool given free/bundled incumbents, which integration surface (proxy vs. plugin vs. provider-native) has real whitespace, and how large the segment with painful (10+) alias counts actually is.\n\n## Regulatory setup\nIf the implementation stores or proxies user email credentials/content, GDPR processor obligations and breach-notification duties apply on an ongoing basis; a one-time legal consult should cover ToS/privacy policy drafting and incident-response planning before launch.\n"
